# PRISM backend configuration.
#
# Copy this file to backend/.env and fill in the key:
#
#   Windows   copy backend\.env.example backend\.env
#   macOS/Linux   cp backend/.env.example backend/.env
#
# .env is read at startup and stays server-side. The browser never sees the key,
# and /api/health reports only whether one is present.

# --- Required ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Get one at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# Without it the API still starts, /api/health answers key_configured: false,
# and POST /api/proposals returns 503 with an actionable message.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Verified model ids: gemini-3.5-flash (default), gemini-3.1-pro-preview,
# gemini-2.5-pro, gemini-2.5-flash, gemini-pro-latest, gemini-flash-latest.
# Flash is the right default here: one call, structured output, images.
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash

# Where generated bundles are written. Relative paths resolve against backend/.
# GENERATED_DIR=generated

# Comma-separated browser origins allowed to call the API. The default covers
# the Vite dev server on both hostnames.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://127.0.0.1:5173

# Bind address and port for `py -m app.main`.
# HOST=127.0.0.1
# PORT=8000

# Upload and input caps.
# MAX_IMAGES=8
# MAX_IMAGE_BYTES=8388608
# MAX_BRIEF_CHARS=20000

# --- Accounts (optional) -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Sign-in is Supabase's. Leave these empty and PRISM works exactly as it always
# has: no sign-in screen, and an API that answers anyone who can reach it. Fill
# them in and the API verifies a Supabase access token on every call - a browser
# without a session gets 401, and so does curl.
#
# To set it up:
#   1. Make a project at https://supabase.com. Any region, free tier is fine.
#   2. Project Settings -> API. Copy the Project URL and the anon/publishable key.
#   3. Paste them below and restart the API. The client reads them from the API,
#      so there is nothing to configure in the frontend.
#   4. Authentication -> URL Configuration. Add your app's origin
#      (http://localhost:5173, or 5174 if that is what you run) as a redirect URL.
#   5. Authentication -> Providers:
#        Email    - on by default. Covers the password form and the six-digit
#                   code; the code screen needs no extra setup.
#        Google   - make an OAuth client in Google Cloud Console, paste the
#                   client id and secret into Supabase, and add Supabase's
#                   callback URL to the client's authorised redirect URIs.
#        Facebook - make an app at developers.facebook.com, add Facebook Login,
#                   paste the App ID and App Secret into Supabase, and put
#                   Supabase's callback URL in "Valid OAuth Redirect URIs". A
#                   Facebook app also has to leave development mode before
#                   anyone outside your own account can sign in with it.
#      A provider that is not configured shows its button and fails on click -
#      Supabase's own message, reported on the screen.
#
# The URL alone is enough: tokens are then checked against the project's
# published keys. SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET is only for a project still using the
# legacy shared secret - it saves a network call and nothing else. Never put the
# service_role key in either field; it is not needed and it is not safe here.

# SUPABASE_URL=https://yourproject.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

# --- Invitations (optional) --------------------------------------------------
#
# The one email PRISM sends: "you have been invited to this workspace". Without
# a key the invite is still created and the app shows you the link to send
# yourself. RESEND_FROM has to be on a domain verified in Resend.

# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# RESEND_FROM=invites@yourdomain.com

# Where invitation links point - your app's origin, not the API's.
# APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5174
